---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteChrome from '../components/SiteChrome.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

/*
 * Refunds and the right of withdrawal.
 *
 * Two different things that get confused with each other, so this page keeps
 * them apart. A refund is a commercial decision made by whoever took the
 * payment — which is never us. Withdrawal is a statutory right that exists
 * whether or not anyone feels like honouring it, and it has a specific,
 * citable way of ending early for digital content. Saying "digital purchases
 * are non-refundable" would be both wrong and, being an unfair term, void.
 *
 * Scope note: this page is about the purchase relationship, which is the same
 * for every app because the store is the same. It deliberately says nothing
 * about what any app collects — that differs per app and belongs on
 * /privacy, where each app has its own section.
 */
const updated = 'August 9, 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Refunds and withdrawal — DeftDay Studio"
  description="Who takes your money for a DeftDay app, how to ask that store for a refund, and the 14-day right of withdrawal in the EU, the UK and Türkiye — including exactly how it ends early for digital content."
>
  <SiteChrome />

  <main class="legal" id="main">
    <p class="eyebrow">DeftDay Studio</p>
    <h1>Refunds and withdrawal</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Two separate things, often muddled together. A <strong>refund</strong> is
      a decision made by the shop that took your money — Google Play or the
      Apple App Store, never us. <strong>Withdrawal</strong> is a right the law
      gives you, and this page says plainly when it applies and when it stops
      applying.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <h2>1. We are not the shop</h2>
    <p>
      Everything we sell is sold through an app store. The store shows the
      price, takes the payment, issues the receipt, holds the payment method
      and — in most countries — is the seller of record for the purchase; your
      receipt names the entity you contracted with. We never see your card
      details and we have no way to move money in your store account.
    </p>
    <p>
      So we cannot issue a refund for a purchase we did not take. What we can
      do, and will: back up a request you make to the store, confirm the facts
      to them, and tell you honestly if we think the store has got it wrong.
      Write to <a href="mailto:legal@deftday.com">legal@deftday.com</a> and a
      person answers.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Asking Google Play for a refund</h2>
    <p>The route, from the device or from a browser:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>In the Play Store app</strong> — tap your profile picture →
        <b>Payments &amp; subscriptions</b> → <b>Budget &amp; order history</b>,
        find the purchase, then <b>Report a problem</b> and pick the reason that
        matches.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>In a browser</strong> —
        <a href="https://play.google.com/store/account/orderhistory" rel="noopener"
          >play.google.com/store/account/orderhistory</a
        >, then the same <b>Report a problem</b> beside the order.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google's own refund page</strong> —
        <a href="https://support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2479637" rel="noopener"
          >support.google.com/googleplay/answer/2479637</a
        >, which is the authoritative description of the process and the one
        that stays current.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Google handles the earliest requests itself and passes later ones on to
      the developer, and the cut-off it applies has changed over time — its help
      page above is the authority, not this one. If Google sends you to us, we
      answer; if it refuses you and you think a statutory right of yours has
      been ignored, tell us and we will say so to them in writing.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Asking Apple for a refund</h2>
    <p>For anything of ours you bought on the App Store:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>In a browser</strong> —
        <a href="https://reportaproblem.apple.com" rel="noopener">reportaproblem.apple.com</a>,
        sign in, pick the purchase, choose <b>Request a refund</b>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>On an iPhone or iPad</strong> — <b>Settings</b> → your name →
        <b>Media &amp; Purchases</b> → <b>View Account</b> → <b>Purchase
        History</b>, then the purchase.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Apple decides these, on its own published policy, and does not tell us why
      one was granted or refused.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Your 14-day right of withdrawal</h2>
    <p>
      If you are a consumer in the EU or the EEA, the UK, or Türkiye, buying at
      a distance, the law gives you <strong>14 days to withdraw from the
      contract without giving a reason and without penalty</strong>. That is a
      right, not a courtesy, and it exists independently of any store's refund
      policy.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>EU / EEA</strong> — the Consumer Rights Directive
        (2011/83/EU, as amended by Directive (EU) 2019/2161), art. 9, as
        implemented in your own country's law.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>UK</strong> — the Consumer Contracts (Information, Cancellation
        and Additional Charges) Regulations 2013, reg. 29–30.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Türkiye</strong> — Law No. 6502 on Consumer Protection, art. 48,
        and the Distance Contracts Regulation (<i lang="tr"
          >Mesafeli Sözleşmeler Yönetmeliği</i
        >, Official Gazette 27 November 2014, No. 29188), art. 9.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. How that right ends early — and only then</h2>
    <p>
      For digital content that is not supplied on a physical medium, the same
      laws let the 14 days end before they are up. It is not automatic and it is
      not a general rule that "digital is final". Under the EU directive
      (art. 16(1)(m)) and the UK regulations (reg. 37) all of the following have
      to be true:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        performance has actually <strong>begun</strong> — the content or feature
        has been delivered to you;
      </li>
      <li>
        it began <strong>with your prior express consent</strong>, given before
        the 14 days ran out;
      </li>
      <li>
        you <strong>acknowledged that by consenting you lose the right of
        withdrawal</strong>; and
      </li>
      <li>the trader confirmed the contract to you on a durable medium.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout">
      <strong>Where that happens is the store's purchase sheet.</strong> The
      consent and the acknowledgement are what Google Play and Apple present at
      the moment you confirm the purchase, and the receipt they email you is the
      confirmation. We are telling you this here as well so that nobody can say
      it was buried. If a store did not put that to you, the right did not end —
      and you should say so when you ask for your money back.
    </p>
    <p>
      One thing this never covers: a purchase that failed, an app that does not
      work, or a feature that is not what it was described as. That is not a
      withdrawal question at all — see §9.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Türkiye, specifically</h2>
    <p>
      Turkish law reaches the same place by a different route, and the
      difference matters if you are arguing about it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Distance Contracts Regulation lists the contracts the right of
      withdrawal does not apply to in its art. 15. The relevant one, art. 15(1)(ğ),
      excludes <i lang="tr"
        >“elektronik ortamda anında ifa edilen hizmetler veya tüketiciye anında
        teslim edilen gayrimaddi mallara ilişkin sözleşmeler”</i
      > — contracts for services performed instantly in an electronic
      environment, or for intangible goods delivered to the consumer instantly.
      A paid tier that unlocks in the app the moment the store confirms the
      purchase is exactly that: an intangible good, delivered instantly, in an
      electronic environment.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the mechanism is the <em>instant delivery itself</em>, not — as in the
      EU version — a consent plus an acknowledgement. But it is not
      unconditional either. It depends on the pre-contractual information
      (<i lang="tr">ön bilgilendirme</i>, art. 5) having told you before you
      bought that this is a contract the right of withdrawal does not cover; the
      burden of proving that is on the seller, and where that information was
      not properly given the withdrawal period does not simply run out on day
      14. The store's purchase sheet is where that information is given, and
      this page repeats it so it is on the record in plain Turkish and English
      both.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing here waives anything. If you are in Türkiye and you think a
      purchase should be reversed, §10 has the two places you can take it.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Cancelling a subscription is not a refund</h2>
    <p>
      They are different actions with different results, and cancelling by
      mistake when you meant to ask for money back is the most common way people
      end up with neither.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cancelling</strong> stops the next renewal. It does not shorten
        the period you have already paid for and it does not return that money —
        you keep the paid features until the period ends, and then it lapses.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A refund</strong> returns money for a period. You ask the store
        for it, using §2 or §3.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You cancel in the store, not in our app, because the store holds the
      subscription: in the Play Store app, profile picture →
      <b>Payments &amp; subscriptions</b> → <b>Subscriptions</b> → the
      subscription → <b>Cancel subscription</b>; on iOS, <b>Settings</b> → your
      name → <b>Subscriptions</b>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A one-off purchase — where an app offers one instead of a subscription —
      has nothing to cancel. It is paid once and it stays bought; the store
      remembers it, so reinstalling or moving to a new phone restores it with no
      account to sign into.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Prices, and changing them</h2>
    <p>
      Prices are set in the store and shown to you in your own currency, with
      whatever tax applies where you live already included. We do not convert
      anything ourselves and we never show a price the store did not give us.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A price change <strong>never applies to a period you have already paid
        for</strong>, and never to a one-off purchase you already own.
      </li>
      <li>
        For a subscription, the store runs the notice: Google Play and Apple
        notify subscribers in advance of an increase, by email and in the store,
        and in a number of countries the increase does not take effect at all
        unless you accept it — where you do not, the subscription ends at the
        end of the current period instead of renewing at the new price. The
        exact notice period and whether acceptance is required are the store's
        rules and depend on where you live.
      </li>
      <li>
        A promotional or introductory price lasts for the period stated at
        purchase and then renews at the standard price, which the store shows
        you before you buy.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>9. If it is broken, this is a different right</h2>
    <p>
      Withdrawal is for changing your mind. If a paid feature does not work, is
      not what it was described as, or stops working, that is a conformity
      problem, and consumer law gives you remedies for it that have nothing to
      do with the 14 days — in the EU under the Digital Content Directive
      ((EU) 2019/770), in the UK under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, and in
      Türkiye under Law No. 6502. Typically: have it put right, or a reduction,
      or end the contract if it cannot be put right.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tell us first —
      <a href="mailto:legal@deftday.com">legal@deftday.com</a> — because most of
      the time we can just fix it, and a fix is faster than a refund. If we
      cannot, we will say so rather than stall, and we will back your request to
      the store.
    </p>

    <h2>10. If you disagree with us</h2>
    <p>
      Write first: <a href="mailto:legal@deftday.com">legal@deftday.com</a>. We
      answer, and we would much rather sort it out directly.
    </p>
    <p>
      If that does not settle it, consumers in Türkiye may apply to the Consumer
      Arbitration Committee (<i lang="tr">Tüketici Hakem Heyeti</i>) or the
      Consumer Court where they live, and consumers in the EU may use their
      national alternative dispute resolution body. None of that is affected by
      anything on this page — as our
      <a href="/terms">Terms of Use</a> also say, where a term of ours conflicts
      with your statutory rights, your rights win.
    </p>

    <p class="sign">
      This page describes how the purchase works and what the law gives you. It
      is not legal advice, and it cannot take away a right your own country
      gives you.
    </p>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .legal {
    width: min(72ch, calc(100% - var(--gutter) * 2));
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 140px 0 80px;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--muted);
    letter-spacing: 3px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .legal h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3rem);
    margin: 8px 0 16px;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .updated {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  .legal h2 {
    margin: 48px 0 12px;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .legal p,
  .legal li {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .legal p + p {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }
  .legal ul {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .legal strong,
  .legal b {
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .legal a {
    color: var(--sun-text);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    /* Two of the links on this page are bare URLs with no space in them
       (play.google.com/store/account/orderhistory and Google's support URL).
       A word that cannot break is a word the column has to widen for, and it
       pushed the whole document 39px past a 390px phone — measured, not
       guessed. Breaking mid-URL is ugly; a sideways scrollbar on a page the
       Play Console links to is worse. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .legal a:hover {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .callout {
    margin-top: 16px;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }
  .sign {
    margin-top: 48px;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
